 "sources": [
  {
   "claim": "1,865 adults aged 16-80; all ages report time passing quickly; age differences very small except for last-10-years question; findings support time-pressure theory; James (1890) attributed acceleration to fewer memorable events",
   "url": "https://researchmap.jp/read0153473/published_papers/1402627/attachment_file.pdf",
   "note": "Friedman & Janssen 2010, 'Aging and the speed of time', Acta Psychologica 134:130-141 (full text checked). Abstract: 'A total of 1865 adults from two countries, ranging in age from 16 to 80'. Exp. 1: 99 New Zealanders (young vs. older); Exp. 2: Dutch internet sample. Items asked how fast the last week, month, year and 10 years passed; age differences only on the 10-year item (about 4% of variance in Exp. 2), about 1% or less for the rest."
  },
  {
   "claim": "Age effect found only on the 10-year item in a sample of 499 aged 14-94",
   "url": "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.2466/pr0.97.3.921-935",
   "note": "Wittmann & Lehnhoff 2005, 'Age effects in perception of time', Psychological Reports 97:921-935. Small-to-moderate effects."
  }
 ],
 "caveats": "Round-2 edits: narration now says 'people' rather than 'adults', because the sample included 16- and 17-year-olds (the paper itself calls them adults). The earlier on-screen list 'Week: similar / Year: similar' was checked against the paper and is accurate (no meaningful age difference for last week, month or year; older people rated the last 10 years as faster). It is now shown as two bar pairs: 'last year' young ~= old, '10 years' old > young. Bar lengths are illustrative, not the paper's means. The novelty/memory hypothesis isn't disproven, just unproven. 'Routine didn't predict it' rests on Friedman & Janssen: their life-change/routine measures did not explain speed-of-time ratings, while time pressure did; it's one paper (two samples, New Zealand and the Netherlands). Narration rounds 1,865 to 'over eighteen hundred'. Wittmann & Lehnhoff (2005) likewise found a modest age effect only for the decade question. Round-5 edit: last line is now 'Your to-do list is what flies.' (same meaning; 'does' was misheard). The 'younger/older' bars are illustrative, not the paper's means.",
